  <page>

    <exercise>
        <title>Conceptual Question</title>
        <p>
            No need to write 5 paragraph essays to answer each question but try to answer each question clearly and 
            precisely. It's OK to criticize the language in some of these questions or what the question is 
            saying in your answers <mdash/> that's part of the exercise.

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Given two events A and B (with timelike separation), explain the difference between the 
                        coordinate time <m>\Delta t_{AB}</m>, the spacetime interval <m>\Delta s_{AB}</m>, 
                        and the proper time <m>\Delta\tau_{AB}</m>. Can the coordinate time ever be equal to the 
                        spacetime interval? Can the proper time ever be equal to the spacetime interval?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A particular event has coordinates (x,t) in one frame. How do you use the Lorentz 
                        transformations to find the coordinates (x<prime/>,t<prime/>) of the event in a different
                        reference frame?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        When using the Lorentz transformations, how do we know which frame is the primed frame 
                        (the <q>moving frame</q>) and which frame is the unprimed one (the one <q>at rest</q>)?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Two twins travel away from each other at relativistic speed. The time-dilation result says that each 
                        twin sees the other twin's clock running slow. So each says the other has aged less. 
                        How would you reply to someone who asks, <q>But which twin really is younger?</q> 
                        (Note: this is not the twin paradox situation)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Spacetime Physics Problems</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        3-14
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        L-8 Note: to make the next problem easier, transform the adequate displacements to solve 
                        for <m>\cos\phi</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        L-9 Solve it using velocity transformation
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        L-14 Note: the idea in part f) is very important. A lot of apparent paradoxes
                        come from the fact that an ideal rigid body cannot exist in relativity
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        5-4 Make sure to explain how everything is consistent. For instance, show that
                        in part c) the distance to the tennis balls reduces to what it should be.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        6-3 Additional instructions: for part a), show that you can pick a range of speeds 
                        (smaller than c) that reverses the order of events 
                        (i.e if P is before Q in the home frame, Q is before P in some rocket frames). 
                        For parts b) and c), show that this does not happen: if P is before Q in the home frame, 
                        it will be before Q in every rocket frame.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
  </page>
  
  <page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Two-observer spacetime diagram</title>
        <p>
            Your boss is on the Earth-Pluto shuttle, which travels at a constant velocity of 0.60 straight from Earth to 
            Pluto, a distance of 5.0 h in an inertial frame attached to the sun. An hour into the flight 
            (according to your boss's watch) your boss sends a laser message to you on earth, asking you to send a 
            wake-up call appropriately timed so that your boss can catch a 1-h nap 
            (as measured on your boss's watch). You immediately reply with the wake-up call 
            and an apology that the call is late, claiming in your defense that the laws of physics prevented a 
            timely response. Draw a two-observer spacetime diagram to answer the following questions:

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                    Why was your boss's request impossible?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How long did your boss sleep (according to your boss's watch) before your message was 
                        received at the shuttle? 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Explicit Solution to the twin paradox</title>
        <p>
            The following problem is based on an explicit numerical example of the twin paradox due to 
            Sir Charles Darwin (grandson of the biologist). On New Year's Day, 3000, an astronaut (A) sets out from 
            Earth at speed 0.8c and travels to the nearest star, <m>\alpha</m>-Centauri, which is just about 
            4 light-years away as measured in the Earth frame of reference. 
            Having reached the star she immediately turns around and returns to Earth at the same speed, 
            arriving home on New Year's Day, 3010, by Earth time. 
            The astronaut has a brother (B) who remains on earth, and they agree to send one another 
            greetings by radar-telephone on every New Year's Day until the traveler gets back.

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                         Show, briefly, that A sends only 6 messages
                         (including the one made on the last day of her trip), whereas B sends 10.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Draw a space-time diagram of A's journey as plotted in the Earth reference frame. 
                        Draw also the world lines of all the radar signals that B transmits. 
                        Verify with the help of the diagram that A (the astronaut) has received only 1 signal up to 
                        the moment of her turnaround and receives the other 9 during the return half of the trip.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Draw another space-time diagram, again in the Earth reference frame, showing the world lines of
                        the astronaut and of all the signals that she sends. Verify that her brother receives one 
                        message each 3 years of Earth time for the first 9 years after the departure, and then receives 
                        3 more messages during the last year, making a total of 6—which is just right,
                        since for the astronaut the trip has taken 3 years each way.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>